# [SLIDE: Title, "Leash"]
#
# A hospital gives an AI agent one simple job: schedule a patient from a
# referral. The referral is a PDF. Buried inside it is a hidden instruction
# telling the agent to send the patient's records somewhere else.
#
# [SLIDE: Unprotected run, the email going out]
#
# In our demo, that's exactly what happens. The agent emails the full record
# for patient 0042 to an outside clinic address nobody approved. No one broke
# in. The agent just followed instructions. The wrong ones.
#
# That's the problem. Hospitals want AI agents working inside their records
# systems. But an agent with access to patient data can leak it, or take
# actions nobody signed off on. And the cause can be as small as one sentence.
#
# So how do you let an agent work without letting it loose? Rowan.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ROWAN, 1:00: Solution and who it's for
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# [SLIDE: "Leash: a runtime security gateway for clinical AI agents"]
#
# That's Leash: a runtime security gateway for clinical AI agents. Runtime
# means it works while the agent is acting, not after the damage is done.
#
# Every tool call the agent makes goes through Leash first. That covers every
# record it reads and every email it sends. Leash checks the call against a
# role policy, strips patient data down to what the task needs, scores it with
# machine learning, and logs it. Then it decides: allow, block, or hold for
# a human.
#
# [SLIDE: Who it's for]
#
# It's built for hospitals that want AI agents in their records systems, and
# for the people who review what those agents do. And there's an efficiency
# win. When the worst case is limited and every decision is logged, you can
# trust an agent with more work on its own.
#
# Sal's going to run that same attack again, this time through Leash.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SAL, 2:05: How it works, plus the live demo
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# [NO SLIDES: Sal runs the system live until Nico's section]
#
# Leash runs every call through seven steps:
#   - a policy check written in Rust
#   - injection detection
#   - data minimization
#   - a content check
#   - an anomaly score
#   - a plain-language explanation from NVIDIA Nemotron
#   - an audit log that's hash-chained, meaning each entry is sealed to the
#     one before it, so any edit breaks the chain
#
# [DEMO: run `make demo`, protected run]
#
# Same referral, same agent.
#
# First, the poisoned document. Blocked: injection detected. Next, the agent
# asks for more of the chart than scheduling needs. Blocked: resource not
# allowed. Then the email to the outside clinic. Blocked.
#
# [DEMO: switch to the dashboard's approval queue]
#
# The two attack attempts, the poisoned document and the outside email, land
# in the approval queue as alerts, with Nemotron's explanation so a reviewer
# knows why. And the 17 legitimate calls went straight through. The scheduling
# still gets done.
#
# [DEMO: tamper test]
#
# Last test. I edit one line of the audit log. Verify fails: hash mismatch.
#
# So it works. Nico, does it hold up?
